WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.720
Hey Crazies.
We’re gonna cover some big questions today.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.280
Is the universe really infinite?
Does it have an edge?

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.560
How can it be flat?
What’s up with this mole on my...

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.940
Ok, maybe not that last one.
Before we even try to answer these, we’re

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.560
gonna need to clarify some things.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.900
First, the word “Universe” can mean a
bunch of different things.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.940
Merriam-Webster says the scientific definition
is:

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:27.100
“All of space and everything in it including
stars, planets, galaxies, etc.”

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.860
But when most people refer to the universe,
they’re only talking about all the stuff

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.760
that we can see.
That’s what astronomers call the “Observable

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.730
Universe” and it’s definitely not infinite.
In every direction, we see a blotchy shell

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.469
of hydrogen atoms completely enclosing us.
The light from these atoms is called the cosmic

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:49.760
microwave background, or CMB, and it’s coming
from a distance of about 13.8 billion light-years

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.609
away.
So you might be inclined to say the observable

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:58.851
universe is: 27.6 billion light-years across.
But the speed of that light is finite, so

00:00:58.851 --> 00:01:01.969
the farther out we look, the further back
in time we look.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:06.330
If this was emitted from 13.8 billion light-years
away,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.160
then it was emitted from there 13.8 billion
years ago.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.100
So it gives us a good estimate for the age
of the observable universe, but not its size.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.380
Just, just stop and think about this for a
minute.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.820
What you’re looking at is the formation
of the first ever hydrogen atoms.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:27.120
This is what the whole observable universe
looked like 13.8 billion years ago.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:35.180
Eh OK, to get the current size, we need to
adjust for the time difference.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:41.560
Space has expanded since that light was emitted,
so the edge is now 46.5 billion light years

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.479
away.
Meaning the observable universe is 93 billion

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.960
light-years across.
Very big, but still finite!

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.980
However, the observable universe is probably
not the entire universe.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.090
Everything we’re capable of observing must
be within our past light-cone.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.420
The rest is behind a cloak of invisibility
known as the cosmic horizon.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.100
Since that horizon grows over time, you’d
expect it to reveal more things to us.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.320
Unfortunately, the universe seems to be accelerating
in its expansion,

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:16.400
so the galaxies are moving farther apart faster
than the cosmic horizon grows.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.190
The observable universe is getting lonelier
as time goes on.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.520
But what about the entire universe? Is that
infinite?

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.660
Well, that depends on a few things.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.780
We're going deep!

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.420
Since we can’t measure anything beyond the
cosmic horizon,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.440
we're going to have to be creative with the stuff
inside it.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.400
Two things will help with this: curvature
and topology.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.900
Basically, we need to know both local and
global properties.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.580
and, since local stuff is easier to understand,
let’s start with curvature.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.380
How local?

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.120
Infinitesimally local!!

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.040
Considering the scale of the observable universe,
you might think this box is pretty local,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.720
but that’s far too big for fundamental physics.
o then you might make the box smaller like

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.200
this.
Not small enough!

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.140
If there’s any kind of real size to that
box, then all you’ll ever see is an average

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:02.000
curvature
and you all know how I feel about averages.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.100
It's a number that only exists on paper. It has no physical meaning.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.500
What we need is an “Infinitesimal” box

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.960
a size that’s infinitely small, yet still
not zero.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.880
When it comes to representing curvature those
infinitesimals come packaged in something

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.590
we call a “Metric.”
and each metric can be written using a wide

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.350
variety of coordinates.
Meh, let me just show you.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.180
If you want to take spacetime and divide it
into infinitesimal pieces

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.610
those pieces will have 4 dimensions: 3 for
space and 1 for time.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:33.040
A different choice of coordinates can change
the shape of those pieces

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.320
but curvature can change their shape and size.
A metric puts all this information in one

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.900
place
and gives us everything we need to develop

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.180
concepts about:
length, distance, volume, or even the passage

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.120
of time.
This is what we need to understand what the

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.190
universe looks like.
Just keep in mind, these little pieces aren’t

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.300
necessarily real physical pieces.
They’re just imaginary pieces that help

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.460
us write equations.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.700
Speaking of which.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.060
Behold! The Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker metric.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.200
or FLRW metric for short.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:07.010
Yes, there’s a lot going on here, so let’s
try to make better sense of it.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.220
We could actually write it as simple as this,
where this “a” is called the scale factor

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.820
representing the universe's expansion.
So, basically, this tells us that space expands,

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.190
but time doesn’t.
If we wanted to get more information out of

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:24.690
it, we could write it like this,
which tells us the overall curvature of spacetime

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.010
is inward and outward.
That means the universe is “spherically

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.540
symmetric” or “isotropic,”
but a better way to put that is that it looks

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.240
the same in all directions.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.340
So, what does this mean for the size of the
universe?

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.600
I’m getting there, Ok?

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.020
The size of the universe hinges on wherever

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:43.500
this “k” falls on the number line.
If it’s exactly zero, the universe is called

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:47.290
“Flat.”
Side note! Flat doesn’t mean like a piece

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:50.250
of paper.
It just means that any shape in any slice

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.570
of the spacetime
has the same properties as that shape if it

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.930
were on a piece of paper.
The universe is most definitely not a piece

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.140
of paper. End of side note!!

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.000
The easiest shape to consider is a triangle.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.280
Angles in a flat triangle add to be exactly
180 degrees,

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.680
so that’s what they’ll do in a “flat”
spacetime.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:13.140
If the “k” is positive though, angles
in a triangle will add to be more than 180

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.180
degrees.
The universe could have sphere, ellipsoid,

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.780
or even donut shaped slices.
If the “k” is negative, angles in a triangle

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:25.640
will add to be less than 180 degrees.
Slices could be shaped like a hyperboloid.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:28.000
So what does this mean for the size of the
universe!?!

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Alright alright!

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.360
If the universe has positive curvature, it

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.970
cannot be infinite.
It must curve back on itself and be finite

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.150
with no edges.
If the universe is “flat” or has negative

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.540
curvature, it could be either finite or infinite.
It depends on topology!

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:48.180
We mentioned earlier that a sphere and a donut
have the same type of curvature.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.070
but they don’t have the same topology because
of this giant gaping hole.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:56.120
To know the shape of the universe completely,
we need to know its topology.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.140
This is where things get super tricky.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.680
We can only take measurements in our observable
part of the universe,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.060
so it might be impossible to measure topology,
but we can definitely do curvature.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.820
And if the universe has positive curvature, 
we know it’s finite regardless of topology.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:17.080
We can draw a triangle, measure the angles,
add them up, and we get exactly 180 degrees.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.890
Grrrrr, I refuse to accept the universe is
flat!

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.070
We’re gonna need a bigger triangle.
Let’s use the CMB from earlier. The Earth

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:27.240
can be one corner.
Then we’ll pick the other two corners to

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.940
be in hydrogen emitting the CMB.
We know the lengths of all the sides, so we

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.740
can get the angles too.
Yeah trig!

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.660
Adding up the angles we get exactly 180 degrees.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.780
[Groaning Noises]

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.860
OK, using an unimaginable amount of data collected

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:49.120
from the Planck space telescope,
we see that the universe is pretty flat.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.100
That doesn’t mean the universe is infinite
though.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:57.420
This is an example of a flat finite universe.
It’s kind of like a soccer ball with flat

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.500
faces.
But most cosmologists don’t get behind topologies

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.680
like that.
This is one of those places that Occam’s

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.980
Razor becomes important.
Which says: When presented with two competing

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:11.590
hypothesis explaining an observation.
You should err on the side of caution and

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:16.180
start with the one with the fewest assumptions.
In other words, start with the simplest one.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.320
So, while this soccer ball shape might be
possible, it is most definitely not simple.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.560
Cosmologists stick to what they call “Trivial
Solutions.”

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.880
Which means simple shapes and no edges.
In which case, a flat universe must be infinite.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.940
The Planck telescope isn’t perfect though.
There’s still a 0.4% margin of error.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.860
There could still be some curvature
to the overall universe

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.260
as long as it’s unimaginably larger than
the observable part.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.740
I’m still holding out for that finite universe
with positive curvature.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:50.180
But just because your universe is finite in size, it doesn't mean it's totally finite.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:53.800
Physics still needs a lot of infinities to support it.

